<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>To draw <m>{4x + 9y \le 54}</m> on a graph:</p><p>Give exact values, for example <m>4.5</m>.</p><p>(a) The boundary line crosses the <m>x</m>-axis at <m>x =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="ans1"/>  and the <m>y</m>-axis at <m>y =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="ans2"/> </p><div/><p>(b) The boundary line is drawn [ solid | dashed ] </p><div/><p>(c) Does <m>(0, 0)</m> satisfy the inequality? [ yes | no ] </p><div/><p>(d) In this book we shade the unwanted side of each line. Which side do you shade? [ the side containing (0, 0) | the side not containing (0, 0) ] </p><div/></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) On the <m>x</m>-axis <m>y = 0</m>, so <m>{4}x = {54}</m> and <m>x = {\frac{27}{2}}</m>. On the <m>y</m>-axis <m>x = 0</m>, so <m>{9}y = {54}</m> and <m>y = {6}</m>. Draw the line through <m>({\frac{27}{2}}, 0)</m> and <m>(0, {6})</m>.</p><p>(b) The sign is less than or equal to: it includes the points on the line, so the line is solid.</p><p>(c) At <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>{4}x + {9}y = 0</m>, and <m>{0 \le 54}</m> is true.</p><p>(d) Shade the side where the inequality is false: the side not containing <m>(0, 0)</m>. Shade what you cannot have, and what is left is what you can.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{27}{2}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>6</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p>solid</p></answer>
    <answer><p>yes</p></answer>
    <answer><p>the side not containing (0, 0)</p></answer>
</stack-static>